WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.290
Film Courage: I think we’ve talked before
about how this industry wants to be able to

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:05.810
work with people who are easy to work with.

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:11.999
So if someone is receiving notes, what are
some tips that they’re resistant (whether

00:00:11.999 --> 00:00:17.000
it’s in their body language or in the key
words that they’re using) and vice versa,

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:18.500
showing that they’re open?

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:24.060
Lee Jessup: You always want to really listen
during a note session.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.199
It’s not your time to defend the work.

00:00:26.199 --> 00:00:31.919
It’s your time to listen, to take physical
notes, to jot down thoughts, don’t defend

00:00:31.919 --> 00:00:33.750
the work.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:39.150
You know I’ve had occasions where writers
were given a note and an executive called

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:41.670
out something that was a problem and the writer
[responded] “It’s not a problem!

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:43.079
I don’t think it’s a problem?

00:00:43.079 --> 00:00:44.770
Do you think it’s a problem?

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:45.910
It’s not a problem!”

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:49.359
That sort of thing is an obvious giveaway
that the writer is not listening.

00:00:49.359 --> 00:00:50.710
It’s listening the to note.

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:53.850
It’s listening to the note behind the note.

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:58.120
If there is something that you think truly,
truly, truly (the execute), the person giving

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:04.010
you notes didn’t get, you can say “Well
I tried to illustrate That with This in this

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:05.400
particular scene.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:06.660
Was that not clear?

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:10.700
You want to always ask those leading questions
to find out where you missed the mark because

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.790
ultimately, the mark was missed.

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:15.220
Can we assume that once and awhile an executive
will miss something?

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:17.590
And yeah, usually they are pretty open to
“Oh?

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:19.329
I didn’t realize that This connects to That?

00:01:19.329 --> 00:01:21.259
Maybe we can do That better.

00:01:21.259 --> 00:01:27.670
It’s usually very easy to tell when a writer
is resistant to a note.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:30.579
When the writer is instantly defensive.

00:01:30.579 --> 00:01:36.200
When the writer becomes sarcastic or passive-aggressive
and there’s a lot of passive-aggression

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.369
that can come out in a note session.

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:39.619
That like “Well!

00:01:39.619 --> 00:01:42.579
I thought this or that…but fine!”

00:01:42.579 --> 00:01:47.909
I’ve seen it in note sessions with my writers
and I’ve given some brutal notes.

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:52.330
And there have been people who have just sat
down and jotted notes and have said “Okay,

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:54.360
let me go back and think about it.”

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:59.280
And there are people who with every note say
“Well, I don’t know if I necessarily agree

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.730
with that but fine!”

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:05.500
So you have those little cues that you know
in a minute.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:11.840
Body language first, dismissal, there can
be a judgment of the individual giving notes.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.220
“Well, you’re a woman, so naturally you’ll
think that.”

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:22.120
I’ve certainly gotten that feedback or anything
that has to do with You’re a Woman.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.120
You’re a Mother.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:26.050
I’ve gotten that “Well, you’re a mother
so clearly you couldn’t relate to that.”

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:27.050
No.

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:29.920
I’m a human being.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.540
For a writer getting notes, try not to dismiss
the note giver because ultimately all that

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:40.200
does is sabotage the relationship because
what you’re saying is, yes you took the

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.770
time, you did the work, you read the script
and now you’re giving me your thoughts but

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:46.970
I’m going to dismiss them because suddenly
something about you makes you not good enough

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:47.970
to give those notes.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:49.410
You’re a mother.

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:50.870
You’re a woman.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:54.360
You’re white, you’re black, you’re whatever
you are.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:59.350
But it’s really important not to dismiss
the note giver (especially not in front of

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:02.970
the note giver).

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:08.570
Oftentimes a writer can receive a note and
be perplexed by the note but the thing then

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:10.930
is to look for the note behind the note.

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:14.910
Okay, you gave me a note about X but does
it really have to do with Y?

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:16.050
Is this what’s bothering you?

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:18.810
You can certainly explore this in a conversation?

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:22.950
You want there to be a dialogue and you want
to built a kind of trust where the note giver

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:26.550
knows you’re listening, you’re paying
attention, you’re taking those notes so

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:28.990
when you say “Oh, there is one thing that’s
bumping me.

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:30.450
Can we talk about that a little bit?”

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:34.240
That’s not defensive, that’s thoughtfulness,
saying “There is the one thing.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:38.160
I agree with your list of 20 other notes,
I totally hear you.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:39.160
We’re good.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:40.640
But there’s the one thing that is here and
it’s bumping me.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:43.290
Can we go back to that for a minute?

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:44.700
What was it about this character?

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:45.700
This scene?

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:46.700
This pivot?

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:48.570
This escalation that bumped you?

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:49.730
What was it that felt inauthentic?

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:50.730
Was it that?

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.900
Could it be something else?”

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:54.000
That to me is dialogue.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.380
And it’s differentiating between shutting
down the note giver which can be done physically.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:03.410
Just, I’m no longer interested.

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:10.180
It can be done verbally or getting into a
more collaborative environment which is what

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:11.290
executives want to work with.

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:14.120
They don’t want a writer to be a Yes-Person.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:19.810
There is a differences between being a Yes-Person
and being collaborative.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:24.230
Being collaborative will require some pushback
on occasion and that’s okay.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:28.570
But you’d better be really thoughtful about
your pushback.

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:31.290
You know, that is somebody people want to
work with.

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:32.860
“You suggested X.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:37.091
I didn’t do X because ultimately I thought
it would effect the script this way BUT the

00:04:37.091 --> 00:04:41.410
note behind the note implied that Y would
work so I tried that this time.”

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:42.450
That’s being collaborative.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:44.760
That’s not being a Yes-Person.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:48.380
And the truth of the matter is that a lot
of writers think that they’re given notes

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:52.070
and the executive just wants them to implement
the note verbatim.

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:55.170
I find that to be very rarely the case.

00:04:55.170 --> 00:05:00.490
If that was it, the executive would be a writer….you
know….they’re trying out ideas.

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:05.200
They’re trying different things and they
want you to try them on for size and see whether

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:07.290
they work or don’t work.

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:11.190
So if there is a note that they gave on a
previous draft that you did not implement,

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:14.030
they want you to be able to answer why you
didn’t implement.

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:16.850
They want you to be able to say “Well I
tried That and the way that it effected the

00:05:16.850 --> 00:05:22.100
script was This…because of That I realized
it didn’t work and I took it out BUT I did

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:24.890
something else to offset your note.”

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:27.500
Film Courage: Wouldn’t they want a Yes-Person?

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:28.500
Lee Jessup: No.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:29.500
Film Courage: Why?

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:33.590
Less Jessup: Because they want the writer
to bring their creative juices to it.

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:42.040
That’s why they hire a writer with a specific
story sensibility, with a particular skill

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:46.340
set, with a particular taste level for a particular
project.

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:47.430
Right?

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:52.390
They want someone who will bring their unique
sensibilities to the project, otherwise hire

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:54.040
a typist.

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:59.520
Get the script, hire a typist and have a typist
type in whatever needs to be typed in.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:04.540
Of course there are a few executives that
are known around town for being really locked

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:08.420
into their notes and they want their notes
implemented the way they want them implemented

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:10.180
and that’s it and it’s not a conversation.

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:18.850
But, by and large, I find that the creative
execs, executive producers, producers, development

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:24.520
execs, all are looking for a creative partner
as opposed to somebody just looking to execute

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:25.520
on the page.

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:27.560
They can get their assistant to execute on
the page.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:28.600
They don’t need you for that.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:34.660
They are much better off giving the material
to their assistant who has a bachelor’s

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:40.280
in English Literature to implement some changes,
then going back and forth with you.

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:41.720
They don’t want you to be a Yes-Person.

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:45.680
They want you to provide your unique story
sensibilities.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:52.770
Your unique writerly touch to make their notes
that can be quite sh*tty come singing off

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:53.770
the page.

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:53.786
QUESTION FOR THE VIEWERS: What’s the worst
note you have received and how did you react?

